    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1509">

                <pb facs="0973" n="857" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg857"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.41/150.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Collector of Customs Cobb to the Secretary of
                        State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">El Paso</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-03-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 1, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>After receiving word from Colonel Avila, commander in Juarez, Mexico, that
                    General Villa had authorized commission to proceed to Chihuahua on regular train
                    which had been held, Percival, Hambleton, Manley, Davidson, Thomason, Worsham,
                    commissioners, and Mrs. Benton, accompanied by Mr. Minnehan, went to Juarez to
                    take train at about 11 a.m. We went to office of Colonel Avila <pb facs="0974"
                        n="858" xml:id="pg858"/> where he and Frederico Gonzalez Garza, his legal
                    representative, with their attendants, received British Consul, Percival,
                    Hambleton, Minnehan and me. They confirmed statement that Villa had authorized
                    passage today but stated that Villa must have done so under misapprehension that
                    Carranza had authorized same although Carranza had not done so in fact. Gonzalez
                    Garza said he would wire report to both Carranza and Villa today but that
                    commission could not proceed without first obtaining permission from
                    Carranza.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Z. L. Cobb</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
